WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.755
Self-storage development looks simple: build a box of small units, then rent them out.

2
00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:09.820
But the money is made, or lost, in a few specific places.

3
00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:16.521
Let's walk through the deal flow with our case: a seventy-eight thousand square foot, climate-controlled facility.

4
00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:21.631
Every storage development moves through the same six stages.

5
00:00:21.981 --> 00:00:26.149
Site and feasibility. Budget. Construction, about fifteen months.

6
00:00:26.499 --> 00:00:29.355
Lease-up, about three years. Stabilization.

7
00:00:29.705 --> 00:00:35.069
And the exit: either sell the stabilized facility, or sell it to a REIT the day it opens.

8
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:38.305
It starts with the trade area.

9
00:00:38.655 --> 00:00:44.727
About a hundred and twenty thousand people live within three miles, with lots of renters and new home sales.

10
00:00:45.077 --> 00:00:52.322
Existing supply is only five and a half square feet per person, against seven to eight nationally, so the area is underserved.

11
00:00:52.672 --> 00:00:58.848
And the site sits on a busy road. Storage is a convenience purchase: people rent where they drive past every day.

12
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:01.619
Next, the budget.

13
00:01:01.969 --> 00:01:09.144
Land is two and a half million. The building is nine and a half million: a hundred thousand gross square feet at ninety-five dollars.

14
00:01:09.494 --> 00:01:15.171
Add site work, soft costs, the developer fee, security technology and contingency.

15
00:01:15.521 --> 00:01:19.678
Then financing, and the cost of losing money while the building fills.

16
00:01:20.028 --> 00:01:25.519
All in, seventeen point seven million, or about two hundred twenty-seven dollars per rentable foot.

17
00:01:25.869 --> 00:01:31.001
Only seventy-eight percent of the gross area is rentable. Hallways and elevators earn nothing.

18
00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:39.567
The capital stack is sixty percent construction loan, about ten point six million, and seven point one million of equity.

19
00:01:39.917 --> 00:01:45.165
The equity goes in first, in the first six months. The loan funds everything after that.

20
00:01:45.515 --> 00:01:49.625
So the equity is at risk the longest, and waits the longest to get paid.

21
00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:53.616
Once it opens, the facility is empty.

22
00:01:53.966 --> 00:01:59.562
Storage fills like a hotel opens: discount first to win customers, then raise rents once they're in.

23
00:01:59.912 --> 00:02:05.926
Occupancy climbs to about thirty percent in year one, sixty in year two, and ninety by the end of year three.

24
00:02:06.276 --> 00:02:14.415
Meanwhile, existing customers get rent increases, called ECRIs, and street rates rise from nineteen to twenty-six dollars a foot.

25
00:02:14.765 --> 00:02:21.626
Until revenue covers expenses and interest, the developer funds the shortfall. That's the lease-up deficit in the budget.

26
00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:29.471
At stabilization, ninety percent occupied at twenty-six dollars a foot is one point eight three million of rent.

27
00:02:29.821 --> 00:02:35.754
Add tenant protection plans and fees, less discounts, for one point eight nine million of revenue.

28
00:02:36.104 --> 00:02:39.111
Operating expenses are only about a third of revenue.

29
00:02:39.461 --> 00:02:45.533
So NOI is one point two three million: a margin near sixty-five percent, one of the highest in real estate.

30
00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:49.772
Now the key test: yield on cost.

31
00:02:50.122 --> 00:02:55.753
One point two three million divided by seventeen point seven million is about six point nine percent.

32
00:02:56.103 --> 00:03:00.456
Stabilized storage might trade at a five point seven five percent cap rate.

33
00:03:00.806 --> 00:03:07.378
That's a spread of about a hundred and fifteen basis points, and a stabilized value around twenty-one point four million.

34
00:03:07.728 --> 00:03:11.571
The spread is the developer's reward for taking construction and lease-up risk.

35
00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:14.468
There are two ways out.

36
00:03:14.818 --> 00:03:19.730
Path one: lease it up yourself and sell in year four, for about twenty-one point four million.

37
00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:29.565
That earns the most profit, about one point four seven times the equity, but the IRR is only about ten and a half percent, because the money is tied up for four years.

38
00:03:29.915 --> 00:03:39.412
Path two: a certificate of occupancy deal. A REIT agrees to buy the building the day it opens, for about two hundred thirty dollars a foot, or seventeen point nine million.

39
00:03:39.762 --> 00:03:49.050
Less profit, about one point two seven times, but in fifteen months, so the IRR jumps to about twenty-four percent, and the REIT takes the lease-up risk.

40
00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.697
That's why many storage developers sell at completion.

41
00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:55.348
What could go wrong?

42
00:03:55.698 --> 00:04:00.388
If rents come in two dollars lower, the hold IRR drops to about six percent.

43
00:04:00.738 --> 00:04:04.779
If hard costs run ten percent over, it falls to about seven.

44
00:04:05.129 --> 00:04:13.407
And the biggest risk isn't in the spreadsheet: a competitor opening nearby during your lease-up. That's why lenders watch the supply pipeline so closely.

45
00:04:13.757 --> 00:04:20.038
At stabilization, the debt yield is about eleven and a half percent, so the takeout loan is there if the plan works.

46
00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.466
To recap: buy where supply is thin and visibility is high.

47
00:04:25.816 --> 00:04:28.475
Budget for the lease-up, not just the building.

48
00:04:28.825 --> 00:04:33.271
And choose your exit: profit by holding, or IRR by selling early.

49
00:04:33.621 --> 00:04:38.869
Now try it yourself. Change the inputs in the case study below, and watch the returns move.
